    {'para': '**264.** A stem ⟨bhavant⟩∗ (to be carefully distinguished from the pres. part. act. ⟨bhavant⟩ of ⟨bhū⟩; the two differ only in accent in Perry’s print: bhávant and bhavánt) is frequently used in respectful address as a substitute for the pronoun of the second person. It is construed with the verb in the third person. Its nom. sing. masc. is ⟨bhavān⟩ (fem. ⟨bhavatī⟩); and ⟨bhos⟩, the contracted form of its older voc. ⟨bhavas⟩, is a common exclamation of address: “you, sir!”, “ho, there!”; and is often doubled.∗∗'},
    {'para': '**265. Derivative stems in ⟨an⟩.** These are made by the suffixes ⟨an⟩, ⟨man⟩, and ⟨van⟩, and are, with one or two exceptions, masc. and neut. only. The stem has a triple form. In the strong cases of the masc. the vowel of the suffix is lengthened to ⟨ā⟩, in the weakest cases it is in general dropped; in the middle cases the final ⟨n⟩ is dropped, and it is also lost in the nom. sing. of all genders. In the neuter, the nom.-acc. pl., as being strong cases, lengthen the vowel of the suffix; the same cases in the dual (as weakest cases) lose ⟨a⟩ — but this only optionally. After the ⟨m⟩ or ⟨v⟩ of ⟨man⟩ and ⟨van⟩, when these are preceded by a consonant, the ⟨a⟩ is retained in all the weakest cases, to avoid too great an accumulation of consonants. Examples: ⟨rājan⟩ m., ‘king’; ⟨nāman⟩ n., ‘name’; ⟨ātman⟩ m., ‘soul, self’; ⟨brahman⟩ n., ‘devotion’.'},
    {'para': '∗ Probably contracted from ⟨bhagavant⟩ ‘blessed’.'},
    {'para': '∗∗ ⟨bhos⟩ loses its final ⟨s⟩ before all vowels and all sonant consonants; thus, ⟨bho bho ṛṣe⟩.'},
