    {'para': '**266. Euphonic rules.** Final ⟨k⟩, ⟨ṭ⟩ and ⟨p⟩ remain unaltered before initial surd consonants; before sonants, whether vowel or consonant, they become respectively ⟨g⟩, ⟨ḍ⟩, ⟨b⟩. Before nasals they may be still further assimilated, becoming the nasals ⟨ṅ⟩, ⟨ṇ⟩, ⟨m⟩. Thus, ⟨parivrāṭ na⟩ becomes either ⟨parivrāḍ na⟩ or ⟨parivrāṇ na⟩; ⟨samyak na⟩ becomes ⟨samyag na⟩ or ⟨samyaṅ na⟩. The latter method is much more usual.'},
    {'para': '**267.** Before initial ⟨h⟩ a final mute is made sonant; and then the ⟨h⟩ may either remain unchanged, or be converted into the sonant aspirate corresponding to the preceding letter; thus, either ⟨samyag hastaḥ⟩ or ⟨samyag ghastaḥ⟩; either ⟨tasmād hastāt⟩ or ⟨tasmād dhastāt⟩. In practice the latter method is almost invariably followed.'},
    {'para': '∗ When a dental mute comes in contact with a lingual or palatal mute or sibilant, the dental is usually assimilated, becoming lingual or palatal respectively. Thus, ⟨tiṣṭhati⟩ from ⟨ti-stha-ti⟩; ⟨rājñā⟩ instead of ⟨rājnā⟩.'},
